.TH "FTTY" "1" "October 2026" "ftty 0.4.3" "ftty Manual"
.SH NAME
ftty \- Ultra\-lightweight minimalist Wayland terminal emulator with
native Kitty graphics protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]ftty\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]] [\f[B]\-e\f[R] \f[I]command\f[R]
[\f[I]args\&...\f[R]]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]ftty\f[R] is an ultra\-lightweight, minimalist Wayland terminal
emulator written in Rust.
.PP
It adheres strictly to the Unix philosophy:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Do One Thing Well\f[R]: \f[B]ftty\f[R] is strictly a PTY terminal
surface.
It provides no built\-in tabs, split panes, or session multiplexing.
Window tiling belongs to the window manager (\f[B]xrwm(1)\f[R],
\f[B]river(1)\f[R]), and session multiplexing belongs to multiplexers
like \f[B]herdr(1)\f[R] or \f[B]tmux(1)\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Minimalist & Bloat\-Free\f[R]: Implemented directly as a native
Wayland client using EGL and OpenGL ES without heavy GUI toolkits (GTK,
Qt, GPUI).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Core Protocol Support\f[R]: Built\-in native Kitty graphics
protocol (APC \f[CR]\(rsx1b_G\f[R]) with zero\-copy shared memory
transfers for terminal tools such as \f[B]yazi(1)\f[R] and
\f[B]image.nvim\f[R], first\-class \f[B]fcitx5(1)\f[R] IME integration
via Wayland \f[CR]text\-input\-v3\f[R], and signal\-driven zero\-flicker
dynamic palette switching.
.PP
When executed without arguments, \f[B]ftty\f[R] starts the user\(cqs
default shell determined by the \f[CR]$SHELL\f[R] environment variable
(or \f[CR]/bin/sh\f[R] if unset).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-a\f[R], \f[B]\-\-app\-id\f[R] \f[I]id\f[R]
Sets the Wayland surface application ID (app\-id).
Defaults to \f[CR]ftty\f[R].
This identifier is used by Wayland compositors and window managers for
matching window rules and layout assignment.
.TP
\f[B]\-T\f[R], \f[B]\-\-title\f[R] \f[I]title\f[R]
Sets the initial window title.
Defaults to \f[CR]ftty\f[R].
Applications running inside the terminal may dynamically update the
title via standard OSC 0 and OSC 2 escape sequences.
.TP
\f[B]\-d\f[R], \f[B]\-\-working\-directory\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]
Specifies the initial working directory for the child process.
If the directory does not exist, \f[B]ftty\f[R] exits with an error.
.TP
\f[B]\-c\f[R], \f[B]\-\-config\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]
Specifies an explicit path to a TOML configuration file.
If omitted, \f[B]ftty\f[R] attempts to load configuration from
\f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ftty/ftty.toml\f[R] or
\f[CR]\(ti/.config/ftty/ftty.toml\f[R].
If the default file does not exist, built\-in defaults are used.
.TP
\f[B]\-e\f[R] \f[I]command\f[R] [\f[I]args\&...\f[R]]
Executes the specified \f[I]command\f[R] with subsequent arguments
directly instead of launching the default login shell.
Must be passed as the last option on the command line.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Prints a summary of command\-line usage and options, then exits.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Prints the \f[B]ftty\f[R] version number and exits.
.SH CONFIGURATION
\f[B]ftty\f[R] uses declarative TOML for configuration.
The configuration file supports modular composition via
\f[CR]include\f[R] directives.
.SS INCLUDES
.TP
\f[B]include\f[R] = [\f[I]\(lqpath/to/theme.toml\(rq\f[R], \&...]
An array of file paths to merge into the current configuration.
Paths may be absolute or relative to the directory of the file
containing the include.
Merged files are evaluated in order, with options in the root file
taking precedence over included values.
Circular includes are automatically detected and prevented.
.SS FONT SECTION
The \f[CR][font]\f[R] table configures typography and glyph rendering:
.TP
\f[B]family\f[R] = \f[I]\(lqmonospace\(rq\f[R]
Specifies a single font family name, or an ordered array of fallback
font families:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][font]\f[R]
family = [\(dqJetBrains Mono\(dq, \(dqNoto Color Emoji\(dq, \(dqmonospace\(dq]
.EE
.PP
\f[I](The alias \f[CI]families\f[I] is also accepted).\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]size\f[R] = \f[I]9.0\f[R]
Font point size as a floating\-point number.
Defaults to \f[CR]9.0\f[R].
.SS WINDOW SECTION
The \f[CR][window]\f[R] table configures surface metrics:
.TP
\f[B]padding\f[R] = \f[I]4\f[R] | [\f[I]4\f[R], \f[I]2\f[R]]
Internal surface padding in logical pixels between the terminal grid and
window borders.
May be specified as a uniform scalar (\f[CR]padding = 4\f[R]) or as a
two\-element array \f[CR][horizontal, vertical]\f[R]
(\f[CR]padding = [4, 2]\f[R]).
Defaults to \f[CR]4\f[R].
.SS CURSOR SECTION
The \f[CR][cursor]\f[R] table configures cursor styling:
.TP
\f[B]shape\f[R] = \f[I]\(lqblock\(rq\f[R] | \f[I]\(lqbeam\(rq\f[R] | \f[I]\(lqunderline\(rq\f[R]
Sets the initial cursor shape.
Defaults to \f[CR]\(dqblock\(dq\f[R].
.SS COLORS SECTION
The \f[CR][colors]\f[R] table defines the color palette:
.TP
\f[B]foreground\f[R] = \f[I]\(lq#dcdcdc\(rq\f[R]
Primary text foreground color in hexadecimal (\f[CR]#RRGGBB\f[R] or
\f[CR]0xRRGGBB\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]background\f[R] = \f[I]\(lq#181818\(rq\f[R]
Surface background color in hexadecimal.
.PP
Standard 16 ANSI colors:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]black\f[R], \f[B]red\f[R], \f[B]green\f[R], \f[B]yellow\f[R],
\f[B]blue\f[R], \f[B]magenta\f[R], \f[B]cyan\f[R], \f[B]white\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]bright_black\f[R], \f[B]bright_red\f[R], \f[B]bright_green\f[R],
\f[B]bright_yellow\f[R], \f[B]bright_blue\f[R],
\f[B]bright_magenta\f[R], \f[B]bright_cyan\f[R], \f[B]bright_white\f[R]
.SS Extended 256\-Color Palette
The \f[CR][colors.palette]\f[R] subtable allows overriding any of the
256 indexed ANSI colors:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][colors.palette]\f[R]
\(dq16\(dq = \(dq#1a1b26\(dq
\(dq240\(dq = \(dq#414868\(dq
.EE
.SS KEY BINDINGS SECTION
The \f[CR][bindings]\f[R] table maps keyboard combinations to terminal
actions:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][bindings]\f[R]
\(dqctrl+shift+c\(dq = \(dqcopy\(dq
\(dqctrl+shift+v\(dq = \(dqpaste\(dq
\(dqctrl+plus\(dq = \(dqincrease_font_size\(dq
\(dqctrl+equal\(dq = \(dqincrease_font_size\(dq
\(dqctrl+minus\(dq = \(dqdecrease_font_size\(dq
\(dqctrl+0\(dq = \(dqreset_font_size\(dq
\(dqctrl+shift+n\(dq = \(dqspawn_terminal\(dq
\(dqctrl+shift+f\(dq = \(dqtoggle_fullscreen\(dq
.EE
.PP
Supported actions:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]copy\f[R]: Copy active text selection to clipboard.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]paste\f[R]: Paste clipboard content to the running PTY.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]increase_font_size\f[R]: Increase current font size dynamically.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]decrease_font_size\f[R]: Decrease current font size dynamically.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]reset_font_size\f[R]: Reset font size to configured default.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]spawn_terminal\f[R]: Launch a new instance of \f[B]ftty\f[R] in the
current working directory.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]toggle_fullscreen\f[R]: Toggle Wayland surface fullscreen state.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
\f[B]SIGUSR1\f[R]
Triggers an immediate, zero\-flicker dynamic configuration and palette
reload.
The configuration file and all included palettes are reparsed, and GL
texture atlases and font faces are dynamically refreshed without
restarting the terminal or interrupting child processes.
.TP
\f[B]SIGWINCH\f[R]
Sent automatically to the child PTY foreground process group when the
Wayland surface resizes or font dimensions change.
.SH PROTOCOLS & EXTENSIONS
.TP
\f[B]Kitty Graphics Protocol\f[R]
Implements native Kitty graphics escape codes
(\f[CR]\(rsx1b_G...;payload\(rsx1b\(rs\f[R]).
Supports action types transmit (\f[CR]a=t\f[R]), transmit and display
(\f[CR]a=T\f[R]), query (\f[CR]a=q\f[R]), delete (\f[CR]a=d\f[R]), and
placement (\f[CR]a=p\f[R]).
Zero\-copy shared memory (\f[CR]t=s\f[R], \f[CR]t=f\f[R]) and direct
transmission (\f[CR]t=d\f[R], \f[CR]t=m\f[R]) modes are supported,
allowing seamless, high\-performance image display in tools like
\f[B]yazi(1)\f[R] and \f[B]image.nvim\f[R].
Responses are formatted strictly in compliance with the Kitty
specification.
.TP
\f[B]Wayland text\-input\-v3 (IME)\f[R]
Fully integrates Wayland \f[CR]zwp_text_input_v3\f[R] protocol for input
method engines such as \f[B]fcitx5(1)\f[R].
Accurately reports cursor rectangle coordinates in surface space to
position candidate popup windows, with native preedit text rendering and
styling.
.TP
\f[B]Kitty Keyboard Protocol\f[R]
Supports progressive keyboard enhancement modes (disambiguate escape
codes, report alternate keys, report all keys as escape sequences) to
eliminate legacy terminal escape ambiguities.
.TP
\f[B]OSC 52 Clipboard\f[R]
Supports reading and writing the system clipboard via standard OSC 52
base64 escape sequences.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ftty/ftty.toml\f[R] or \f[CR]\(ti/.config/ftty/ftty.toml\f[R]
Default user configuration file location.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]FTTY_CONFIG\f[R]
Optional path to an alternative configuration file.
.TP
\f[B]TERM\f[R]
Terminal type identifier exported to child processes.
Set to \f[CR]xterm\-256color\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]COLORTERM\f[R]
Set to \f[CR]truecolor\f[R] to announce 24\-bit direct RGB color
capability.
.TP
\f[B]WAYLAND_DISPLAY\f[R]
Names the Wayland compositor socket used for connection.
